In article <3s9d8c$p8h@sloth.swcp.com>, phdewi@swcp.com (Phillip Williams) writes:

:I am having a problem with my cluster. I have  4 VAXs II 3 2000s and
:a 3100. All are at VMS 5.1 with DECnet 5.0. The problem is the 3100
:will not join the cluster. I keep getting unable to locate SYSLOA42D.exe
:but when I boot the 3100 and do a dir the file is clearly there. Also
:the 3100 will try to boot off of ESA0 but it looks like its in a loop.

  The VAXstation 3100 models 30 and 40 require V5.1B or later, or
  V5.3-1 or later for a VAXstation 3100 equipped with the SPX graphics
  controller.  The VAXstation 3100 models 38 and 48 require V5.2-1 or
  later, or V5.3-1 or later for the systems equipped with the SPX. 
  The 3100 model 76 requires V5.4 or later.

  DECnet is not used for clustering, only for the initial download
  of the operating system.  The SCS protocol is used for clustering.

  Enable OPCOM messages (REPLY/ENABLE) on a terminal on the boot host,
  and see if anything interesting appears during the download.

  The phrasing "will not join the cluster", and "unable to locate
  SYSLOA42D" but "I do a DIR and there it is" leads me to believe
  that you have a bootable VMS system root on the VAXstation 3100,
  and that you can boot the 3100 off the local disk, and that you
  cannot boot the 3100 as a satellite of another member -- the boot
  node -- of the cluster. 

  Given that the 3100 is significantly faster than the other systems
  in this cluster, I would bring the VAXstation 3100 into the cluster
  running off the local system disk -- I would not run the system as
  a satellite.  This requires that the version of VMS running on the
  3100 is compatible with the version running in the rest of the cluster.

  Upgrading to V5.2 will probably solve the current problem.

 
  ------------------------------ Opinionative -------------------------------
   Stephen Hoffman      OpenVMS Engineering      hoffman@xdelta.enet.dec.com
