In article <DESNOGUE.95Jun23120746@alto.unice.fr>, desnogue@unice.fr (Laurent.DESNOGUES) writes:
>From: desnogue@unice.fr (Laurent.DESNOGUES)
>Newsgroups: comp.sys.dec
>Subject: Wanted:  Some information about old DECs
>
>I'd like to get some information about these models:
>	DEC 5900 [ DECsystem 5900 ]
>	DEC 5240 [ DECsystem/DECstation 5000 Model 240 ]

These two used the same base system enclosure, with the 5900 variant
mounted in a cabinet.  The cabinet include drawers for SCSI disks.
The 5900 may have included a Prestoserve NVRAM and license as part
of the system.  These used an R3000A running at 40 Mhz, hence the
40 in the 240 model number.

>	DEC 5200 [ DECsystem/DECstation 5000 Model 200 ]

The original DECstation/DECsystem 5000 model.  It had the CPU built
into the system module, where all the others of the 5000 line used a
mother-board/daughter card for the CPU.  This arrangement made the
R4000 upgrades easier.  The Model 200, 240/260 and 5900 used the
same kind of memory, MS02.  The Model 200 had a 25 Mhz R3000.

>	DEC 5033 [ Personal DECstation/DECsystem 5000 Model 33 ]
>	DEC 5020 [ Personal DECstation/DECsystem 5000 Model 20 ]

The Personal DECstation/DECsystem familiy used CPU block speed for
the model number.  So a Model 33 was a 33 Mhz CPU.  Slower clock
speeds may have been plain R3000s, with the higher speeds being
R3000As.

The Model 100s and Personal systems used MS01 memory, same as
used in the DECstation 3100.  The Personal DECstations had
the a graphics subsystem built into the base system, where
the other systems used TURBOchannel adapters for graphics.
I don't know whether the server system based on the Personal
DECstation just didn't use it, had it removed, didn't have a
to hook cables or what.  I think I've seen a Personal DECstation
once...

>What I'd like to know is:  which of these are servers (guess the 33 and
>20 are stations) and what the standard processor and frequency on each
>of these machines (supposing no upgrade, of course) are.

If the system uses a plain terminal for a console, then it is a
server.  If it uses a graphics subsystem, then it is a workstation.
Not that a workstation can't be file server, but if it gets heavily
used, the graphics subsystem user may not appreciate the performance
drain.

All the 5000 series used the TURBOchannel for graphics, network
and storage options.  The TURBOchannel in the Personal systems
and Model 100 systems was 12.5 Mhz (50 MB/sec), while the Model
200s and 240 used a 25 Mhz bus (100 MB/sec).  After the Model 200
all the built-in SCSI adapter supported DMA.

