In article <DAxxAM.B7r@bath.ac.uk>, bsspak@bath.ac.uk (P A Keller) writes:
=Hi everyone. Can anyone explain why the following minimal code won't
=compile without error under DEC C?

It's not giving you an error, it's giving you a warning.

=VAX C is fine, as well as DEC C with the
=/STANDARD=VAXC option. As far as I can see, there is nothing here which
=conflicts with the example in the DEC C documentation, or am I just being
=dim?
=
=#include <rms.h> 
=
=struct FAB infab; 
=struct XABFHC inxab; 
=
=void func (void) { 
=
=   infab = cc$rms_fab; 
=   inxab = cc$rms_xabfhc; 
=   infab.fab$l_xab = &inxab; 
=}
=
=produces
=
=   infab.fab$l_xab = &inxab; 
=....^ 
=%CC-W-PTRMISMATCH, In this statement, the referenced type of the pointer value
="&inxab" is "struct xabfhcdef", which is not compatible with "char". 
=at line number 10 in file DKA100:[USERS.PETER.CIF]RMS.C;5 

Well, as the error message tells you, you've got a pointer mismatch.  The
compiler is warning you about that.  If you don't want the warning, you've got
to let the compiler know that you know there's a mismatch, but want to make th
assignment anyway.  The way to do that is by casting the reference to the
pointer to the XAB:

#include <rms.h> 

struct FAB infab; 
struct XABFHC inxab; 

void func (void) { 

   infab = cc$rms_fab; 
   inxab = cc$rms_xabfhc; 
   infab.fab$l_xab = (char *) &inxab; 
}
