In article <3srdof$2v6@pheidippides.axion.bt.co.uk>, thomasgd@boat.bt.com (Greg Thomas) writes:

>In the very near future, the project I work on will have to upgrade to 
>DECnet/OSI. However, the rest of the companies systems will remain at Phase IV 
>for now. I have heard bandied about the phrase "DECnet/OSI using Phase IV 
>protocols". Does this just mean that the router automatically changes the 
>addressing to a Phase V standard when a phase IV packet is aimed at a Phase V 
>box, or does DECnet/OSI support Phase IV protocols out of the wire in the back 
>of the box?

I don't understand the inner workings of DECnet/OSI, but in practice it's all
very simple.  When you install DECnet/OSI it will ask you "do you want to use
Phase IV style addressing?", or something like that, to which you answer yes. 
You then have to do things like give you Phase V nodes Phase IV aliases (eg.
Give Phase V node ".local.alberteinstein" a Phase IV alias "albert").  It's all
quite straightforward though, and is just part of the NET$CONFIGURE routine
that you have to do after installation.  The system then works automatically
with your existing Phase IV nodes.

We have a cluster of three OpenVMS Alphas running DECnet/OSI Phase V, a couple
of creaking VAXs running DECnet Phase IV, and an Digital UNIX Alpha running
DECnet/OSI Phase V.  It all works perfectly!

