DECnet/OSI maintains a cache of information on what systems talk OSI, IP, and
Phase IV and communicates in the appropriate protocol. It's quite automatic.
This applies to both end nodes and routers, so it's all fairly
straight-forward. The one caveat is routing protocols. As soon as an end-system
sees an IS hello, it will switch to OSI and start talking to routers using OSI
protocols. But the router will still talk Phase IV protocols to end systems
that don't run DECnet/OSI.

>If this is true, I can then save the time and money upgrading/replacing 
>our existing routers to support Phase IV/OSI changes, and wait until the rest 
>of the company catches up.

This is often a matter of topology. All level 1 routers in an area must run the
same pouting protocol. So if you are in an area of your own, you can convert to
DECnet/OSI at any time. If you share an area with others who will not be
converting, you will have a problem.

>Alternatively, can anyone point me to a summary of Phase IV/OSI interworking?

Digital publishes a number of handbooks discussing DECnet/OSI, but most are a
bit outdated since they were written in early DECnet/OSI days prior to the
introduction of many DECnet/OSI features including GUI management interface,
networking over TCP/IP, large local name space, BIND name space, and many
others.

