First, I'll assume you are really asking about OpenVMS and not VAX specifically.
Probably the best introductory book is:

              OpenVMS User's Manual - AA-PV5JB-TK

              Provides an overview of the operating system and presents
              basic concepts, task information, and reference information
              that enable you to perform daily computing tasks. It
              describes how to work with files and directories. It also
              includes these additional topics:

              o  Sending messages with the Mail utility and the Phone
                 utility

              o  Using the Sort/Merge utility

              o  Using logical names and symbols

              o  Writing command procedures

              o  Editing files with the EVE and EDT text editors

              o  Processing files with DIGITAL Standard Runoff (DSR)

If you're interested in programming, then you'll want:

              OpenVMS Programming Environment Manual - AA-PV66B-TK

              Provides a general description of Digital products and
              tools that define the programming environment. This manual
              introduces facilities and tools such as the compilers,
              the linker, the debugger, the System Dump Analyzer, system
              services, and routine libraries.

              OpenVMS Programming Concepts Manual - AA-PV67B-TK

              Describes concepts such as process creation, interprocess
              communication, data sharing, condition handling, and ASTs.
              This manual uses system services, utility routines, and
              run-time library (RTL) routines to illustrate mechanisms
              for utilizing OpenVMS features.

These are orderable from Digital (1-800-DIGITAL in the US).

Many people, however, find that the online HELP is sufficient as detailed
in the FAQ.  OpenVMS is easier to learn and start being productive on than some
other systems.
