In article <3ss8v4$fp5@newsbf02.news.aol.com>, aadams8333@aol.com (AAdams8333)
 writes:
:We are a healthcare provider and are currenly running a VAX 4200.  We have
:out grown the system.  The problem is all of our current software was
:written in-house and in DIBOL.  Need info from someone who has maybe
:'been there' and would have some good suggestions on moving to a new
:system, but running our current software.

  I'll assume your requirements have outgrown the CPU performance,
  and not the storage and/or memory...

  In general, one can freely transfer non-privileged-mode software
  across any member of the VAX family running the same version of
  OpenVMS.  And one can also upgrade non-privileged-mode software
  to newer VAX systems and to newer versions of OpenVMS.  (There
  are images around here that were built under then-pre-release
  baselevels of VMS (back circa 1978), and they still run fine.)

  Privileged-mode (ie code that is executing in executive or kernel
  mode) can require rework ranging from a recompile and relink to a
  complete redesign, depending on the sorts of linkages it makes into
  OpenVMS inner-mode routines.

  Reasonable choices are the VAX 4000-200 to VAX 4000-50 upgrade, and
  the VAX 4000-200 to VAX 4000-106A upgrade.  Both are significantly
  faster than the '200, and both allow you to continue using most (all?)
  of the Q-bus peripherals you might have installed in the '200.  (If
  you are not using any `odd' Q-bus perpherals, there are other upgrades
  available, too.)  And, of course, you can cluster a suitable new system
  with the existing '200 via Ethernet or via DSSI.

  You will want to contact the DIBOL vendor and ask if they are aware
  of any problems with the package on the prefered target system(s).
  The '50 and '106A are supported by OpenVMS VAX V5.5-2 and V5.5-2H4
  respectively, and both by OpenVMS VAX V6.1 and later.

  ------------------------------ Opinionative -------------------------------
   Stephen Hoffman      OpenVMS Engineering      hoffman@xdelta.enet.dec.com
