In article <3smuem$8ge@cerberus-138.wustl.edu>
ponder@dasher.wustl.edu (Jay Ponder) wrote:

> I'm trying to get the Logical Storage Manager (LSM) to behave on an Alpha 2100.
> All I want to do is to set up three or four volumes, each of which is the
> concatenation of two or three whole small disks. The man pages for the many
> utilities have left me somewhat confused, so does anyone out there have any
> insight into the following questions (in no particular order):
> 
> (1) Given the simple objective above how should my disks be configured under
>     LSM; the main choices appear to be "simple" and "sliced" (gee... I
>     really wanted mine diced...)?

I use voldiskadm option 1 (Add or initialize a disk) and add (for instance)
rz3, rather than rz3c: the disk name, rather than a partition name.

See below.

> (2) I finally figured out how to "delete" a disk from a disk group (you have
>     to remove all the corresponding subdisks from volumes first...). Now
>     does anyone know how to "delete" a plex or a volume that I no longer
>     want.

voldiskadm option 3 (Remove a disk) or "voldisk rm"
> 
> (3) If I get REALLY frustated, can I simply back up all my stuff to tape,
>     and completely reconfigure LSM from scratch? If so, what files need
>     to be deleted from my system in order to "start over" (the stuff found
>     in /etc/vol/... ?, the stuff in /dev/vol/... ? , anything else ?) 

I think /etc/vol is the place.

> (4) Since I already had some disk partitions on the system, I "encapsulated"
>     them to get them into LSM. It seems LSM automatically gave names to the
>     new plexes/volumes. Can these names be changed to fit way we tend to
>     name such things locally?

voledit rename

> (5) When I tried to add a new disk to the volume resulting from the disk
>     "encapsulation" in (4), it seems to increase the length of the volume
>     as shown by volprint, but I can't get the space to show up on mounting
>     the volume? I've gotten this to work upon adding a disk to other
>     volumes that did not arise via encapsulation. Is the problem related
>     to the fact that the encapsulated volume is "complete"?

Changing the size of the volume has no bearing on what the file system
thinks is there. Once you have used newfs or mkfdmn/mkfset, you have
fixed the size of the file system, no matter what happens to the volume
afterwards. If the volume gets bigger, you're wasting space. If the volume
gets smaller, you're likely to crash sooner or later.

> (6) Does anyone have a suggestion as to what (minimal) set of stuff I
>     should read to figure out LSM? The man pages are confusing, and when
>     I tried to print the online doc manuals, I literally was presented
>     with a 3 inch thick stack of paper just for LSM!

Not sure about this: even volintro(8) and vol(7) get very deep very fast.
8-)

> As an aside, it appears to this novice end-user that LSM goes to far to
> great lengths to "protect" us from doing something "bad". For example,
> sometimes you just want to DO something and you don't care if you might
> lose the data since you already have three backups on tape....

I use voldiskadm for adding disks, and then use dxlsm for everything else.
If you can figure out the distinction between disks, subdisks, plexes,
volumes, and filesystems, things come a bit easier.

> Note to DEC: Why is this so hard? Any help appreciated, as I slowly pull
> out my remaining hair here in St. Louis....

It's hard because it's complicated. A bicycle and a 747 both provide the
same basic functionality (getting from A to B), but a 747 isn't quite as
easy to figure out as a bicycle is, because it offers a lot more than a
bicycle does.

Having said that, the number of commands and options provided by LSM does
seem daunting, because both "top down" and "bottom up" commands are
provided. That's why I stick to dxlsm.

To answer your particular problem, use voldiskadm to add the disks
(rz3, rz4, rz5, not rz3c, rz4c, rz5c), then use dxlsm to create a volume.
From memory, it's just a Basic Add Volume Concatenated (or Striped if
you like) menu option, tell it how many MB/GB you want in the volume,
and it will build it for you. Then mkfdmn/mkfset on the volume.

PJDM
