In article <3secmb$oed@Trex.IenD.wau.nl>, erik.molenaar@net.iend.wau.nl (Erik Molenaar) writes:
>Hi,
>
>LAT-Users in our network that are at some distance, keep getting disconnected 
>from our VAXes, while TCP/IP users have no problem at all. Is this normal for 
>LAT? 

It's normal if you have a lot of packet loss or delay.

>
>Could heavy network traffic cause these disconnects? Does LAT have critical 
>timers?
>

LAT circuits between servers and hosts have a keepalive timer of about
30 seconds.  If there's no activity on the circuit for that time
period, all sessions are dropped.  Null packets are periodically sent
over circuits that are idle.  TCP/IP sessions don't have this feature
and are less sensitive to net problems.

You need to determine why LAT packets might not be getting from one
end to the other.  Since you said the users are at "some distance",
you probably have some bridges.  Maybe they can't handle the load.

>Thanks!
>
>Erik Molenaar
>
>

Mike Wright
University of Florida



