sarkar@ponder.csci.unt.edu (Falguni Sarkar) writes:
>I am using a DEC Alpha machine. When I try to compile the
>following code I am facing problems. 

>#include <stdio.h>
>#include <time.h>

>time_t tim1,*tp;
>main(){
>   tim1=time(tp);
>   if(tp==NULL)
>     printf("tp is NULL\n");
>}

>If I use cc then tp is always returned as NULL. 

Mr. Sarkar, the problem stems from the fact that time() cannot modify
the value of the pointer tp; it could only change the copy it has.
The argument of time, if non-NULL, is where a the time value is
stuffed, in addition to being returned, otherwise the argument is
ignored completely.  To get the time, either:

          time(&tim1);

or:

          tim1=time(NULL);

will work just fine and return the time to you in the variable tim1.


>Thanks in advance,
>Falguni Sarkar.


Best regards,


