keywords: ALPHA,CC,GCC, time
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

sarkar@ponder.csci.unt.edu (Falguni Sarkar) writes:

>I am using a DEC Alpha machine. When I try to compile the
>following code I am facing problems. 

>#include <stdio.h>
>#include <time.h>

>time_t tim1,*tp;
>main(){
>   tim1=time(tp);
>   if(tp==NULL)
>     printf("tp is NULL\n");
>}

It should read...

(appropriate #includes)

main()
{
   time_t  tim1, tim2;

   time(&tim1);

   /* do the stuff to be timed */

   time(&tim2);

   printf("Took %d seconds to run\n", tim2 - tim1);
}

The parameter passed to time() needs to be the address of some storage.
Just declaring it as "time_t *tp;" does not alocate any storage, it just
declares tp to be a pointer to a time_t (an unsigned long int). Time() is
pretty low resolution. It only returns whole seconds. You might want to
try some of the higher resolution methods that Ive seen posted here.
EMail me for copies if you need them.

Hope this helps!
