  I recently ordered an AlphaStation 250 "Base system", without
memory or hard drive ( intending to install 3rd party memory and
Digital DSP disk drives ). After about a month had elapsed the vendor
checked on the order and was told that Digital (Canada) had refused to
accept the order because there was no disk and memory on it.

  I then looked more closely at the SOC and I see that these items
are listed as "required". There is no explanation of what "required" means
though (SOC Jan  1995). I would have presumed that this simply means that
the system won't work without memory or a hard drive ( not completely
true in the hard drive case of course, but "close enough" in that
most people would be crazy to try to run one without a local page/swap
device). It seems though that Digital means "won't accept the order 
unless you buy these from us as well".

   This strikes me as classic "bait and switch" marketing, ie offer
me a competetively priced system and then demand that I buy overpriced
(as compared to competitors equivalent product) memory and disk drives
for it. I thought the "kindler, gentler" Digital didn't engage in these
sort of shenanigans anymore.

    Have others had the same experience or am I just being given the
run around by a sales manager who hasn't yet been "reeducated".

    (ps the vendor has agreed to make good on the original quote by
buying these items from Digital and then removing them and crediting
me for them)

