From: wasser@ranger.enet.dec.com (ranger::wasser)
Subject: Re: PATHWORKS for WNT licensing questions
Date: 1995/06/20
Message-ID: <3s5knr$84c@nntpd.lkg.dec.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 104726179
x-posted-via: NEWS-NOTES gateway (a hack!)
references: <3rpura$2on@blackice.winternet.com>
organization: Digital Equipment Corporation
newsgroups: vmsnet.networks.desktop.pathworks,comp.sys.dec.micro,comp.sys.dec
x-nn_ref: 40001EB0 E76F47AA 009921C3

> We've bought 150 Pathworks DOS and Windows client licenses.

	Since V4.1 was called "PATHWORKS for DOS" I assume you mean
	you bought 150 PATHWORKS V5.0 (or V5.1) for DOS and Windows
	licenses.

> We buy Windows NT and upgrade PATHWORKS to version 5 and start asking 
> questions about licensing for the new NT clients. 
>
> DEC's response: buy new licenses. 

	I don't think it is too unreasonable to expect to pay for new
	software when you change operating systems.  Would you expect
	a vendor of Unix software to give you a free copy of VMS software
	if you decided to change operating systems?

	Perhaps you should ask Microsoft why NT can't run all your
	existing DOS and Windows applications.Huh?  

> What do WE get for our $30,000+?  NOTHING runs on the NT client!    
> There is no add-on module for the VAX server for it to support NT client   
> attachments.  Just the same server (which, incidently, we pay separate   
> maintainance for).  So what's my $30,000 get me? 

	I assume you bought 150 Combined Client and Server (CCS) licenses
	for the combination of the PATHWORKS for DOS and Windows client 
	and the PATHWORKS for xxx (LAN Manager) servers.  If so I think you
	can use those licenses for connecting from Windows NT as long as
	you are not running PATHWORKS for NT (you don't need DECnet, do you?).
	You will not be using the "PATHWORKS for DOS and Windows" half of
	the license.

	Get the FPA License kit (fpa*.exe and fpa*.txt) from:

		ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/DEC/PCI/

	This kit should include the Client License Transponder and Client 
	License Requester for Windows NT.  You will need to configure it
	to request the CCS license instead of the default FPA license.

> Incidently, I understand that with Pathworks server-based licensing (as   
> opposed to operating-specific client licenses) that the operating system   
> of the client connection is not checked when the client attaches to any   
> of the server's connections.  Is this true?  Because ... 

	The FPS (Server based concurrent use license) and FPA (Client
	based server use license) licenses don't care what client uses
	them.
 
> If I can convert my client licenses, all 150 of them, to one big server   
> license with the same capacity, can I get around the NT-specific issues   
> that way? 

	I'm not sure but I would not expect a conversion from CCS to FPS
	to be available since the pricing and capabilities are so different.
	It is more likely that you could split your CCS licenses into an
	equal number of FPA and CNS licenses.  Even without spliting, the
	NT clients can use the DOS CCS license just like an FPA license.
 
> Am I confused, wrong, or just plain screwed, 

	Like many people you are confused by PATHWORKS licensing.  Just
	remember that a CCS license is an FPA and CNS license glued
	together.



