	Paul,


	As referenced in a couple of earlier notes, Digital does in fact
provide tools to help you with this.  DECmigrate for OpenVMS, aka VEST,
translates VAX/OpenVMS executables to Alpha/OpenVMS executables.  VEST, as
it's most commonly known, can translate most classes of apps; examples of
apps it' can't translate are privileged code, user-written system services,
linked such that code and data are tied to fixed addresses, linked prior to
OpenVMS V4.0, etc.  VEST even handles code that generates code on the fly.


	In addition, many customers buy VEST just for the terrific analysis it
does.  What most folks do is to translate exe's to get to Alpha quickly, and
then go back and port the sources.  This is recommended for the best
performance.  At any rate, VEST can aid folks in both phases of their
migration.  Using VEST analysis features you can pinpoint areas that may give
you trouble in porting (architectural dependencies), areas that may be slow
(unaligned data), and so on.  For $99 (it's been at this price for some time
and I don't believe we have any plan to change the pricing structure), it does
a lot.


	The order number is QL-MWMA9-AA, if you're interested.  Don't call me
though, I'm not a salesperson.  ;-)


	Hope this helps,


