In article <1995Jun12.215309.3086@mec.ohio.gov>, duncan@mec.ohio.gov writes...
>Here is a question for someone who has programed in VAX BASIC before.  Is there
>anyway to execute DCL commands from inside of a basic program?  What I want
>to do is to create a menu in basic and have users execute DCL commands.

The easiest method is to call LIB$SPAWN. This can easily be done 
from any VMS programming language, including BASIC.

It is a little expensive in that it creates a second process to do
the work and the main process is still sitting there 'hogging' 
memory untill the sub process is done.

To use a single process, check out LIB$DO_COMMAND, but this reliquishes 
control to the next image, stopping the menu image. That next image then
has the responability to chain back to the menu driver. Which then has
to re-open its files and so on, not 'cheap' either.
VAX BASIC makes LIB$DO_COMMAND easy through the CHAIN command.
You might want to enure going back to the menu for unexpected
menu endings by running the menu in a loop from a little command file.

	$ON ERROR... <fill in>
	$loop_to_menu = 1
	$loop:
	$RUN MENU
	$IF loop_to_menu THEN goto loop  ! Set to false by menu when done
	$<fill in clean up action>

If excursions from menu to target programs become relatively frequent
(more then once every 10 seconds?) then you may want to keep the spawned
subprocess alive and feed it new commands through a mail box, or more
closely integrate target programs and menu (calling routines in (shareable)
libraries, or relocating some menu actions to the programs.

For real serious implementations, expecting many (hundreds) of users selecting
from dozens of tasks you may want to examine heavy duty solutions such
as Digital's ALL-IN-1 or ACMS or somesuch.

$BASIC

Help chain 

CHAIN

   The CHAIN statement transfers control from the current program to  another
   VAX  BASIC  program.  The program to recieve control must be in executable
   format.

Hope this helps,		+--------------------------------------+
