pc@cup.hp.com (Paul Chan) wrote:
>I need information of how to migrate from DEC stations and VAXes to the new
>Alpha OSF1 and OpenVAX systems.  Do I need to recompile and relink everything?
>Can I move the executables over and expect it to run?  If I don't have the 
>source files any more, can I move object files (.o files (.o) and link them 
>with native libraries and object files?
>
>Any information and pointers are greatly appreciated.


Paul,

If you have the sources then you should recompile and relink. 
Intelligent code WILL do so without any need for changes. Stupid code
(ie FORTRAN common blocks that starts with an integer*2 and is followed
by an array of umpteen thousand real*8) should be slightly modified.
This is 64 bit technology. Performance requires 64 bit boundaries.

Stupid compilers like VAX C is even supported with crippling qualifiers
for the DEC C compiler. 

If you don't have the sources then use VEST a.k.a DECmigratem which does
a pretty decent job, and even have a learn-as-you-go mechanism for
improving performance over time.

VAX VMS can be VEST'ed to Alpha OpenVMS, Ultrix and be DECmigrate'd
to OSF/1 a.k.a Digital Unix. No cross-OS tools, unfortunately.

Unless, of course, you look at the new Solaris -> Digital Unix binary
translator. In due course there might be one for HP-UX too. Then you
can get on with moving your legacy applications to a real computer :-)

Sorry, couldn't resist. Where is HP's DomianOS -> HPUX translator,
or the UX/68K -> UX/PA translator ?! 

Cheers


Bernhard
