In article <3r6qsp$7a6@ankh.iia.org>, smselick@ios.com (Steven Selick) writes:
:Hello. Is it possible to send X display info from a unix machine to a 
:Vaxstation 3100? They are networked together.

  One can ship X displays around over DECnet or TCP/IP transports.  You
  will obviously need to find a common transport among the platforms.
  I've been using both DECnet and DEC TCP/IP (UCX) for a transport and
  have encountered no problems...

  If you choose TCP/IP, you'll need to configure the X Server on the
  OpenVMS system to load the IP transport interface during the X Server
  bootstrap -- in SYS$STARTUP:SYLOGICALS.COM, you will want to issue a
  "define/sys/exec decw$install_tcpip true" command.  (You can likely
  define this logical both live and in SYLOGICALS, and then issue a
  "@sys$startup:decw$startup.com RESTART" command to restart X.  This
  will avoid rebooting, and SYLOGICALS will cause X to load the TCP/IP
  transport after any subsequent system reboot.)

  [And you will need an IP package and license, or a DECnet license.]

