In article <1995Jun6.185706@ivos02> Steve <gassner_st@ivos02> writes:
>
> In general I have had very bad experience using ESC c on most devices, since
> it causes the hardware to pause for a couple seconds.  At least on a
> terminal...  Now there is a terminal/printer DEC Standard  somewhere that I 
> used to know about, and it ustabe on-line even -- but where is it??


There are two distinct ways to issue a reset command to most DEC equipment.

Mnemonic Command              Sequence (7-bit)    Explanation
-------- -------------------  ------------------  -----------------------------

DECSTR   Soft terminal reset  Escape [ ! p        sets terminal to power-up
                                                  default states

RIS      Hard terminal reset  Escape c            replaces all terminal set-up
                                                  parameters with NVR values or
                                                  power-up default values if
                                                  NVR values do not exist.

("VT220 Programmer Pocket Guide" EK-VT220-HR-001, page 33)

 =-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=-=

DEC printers, as well as DEC video terminals, typically are controlled
by a set of codes that form a superset of the ANSI X3.64 (ISO DP-6429)
standard.

I keep a collection of informal summaries of the control sequences for
the VT100, VT220, and other DEC terminals. available for public access
on the Internet, either by anonymous FTP or by World Wide Web browser.

By FTP:

    Connect to CS.UTK.EDU [128.169.94.1];
    login in with username of "anonymous" and with the password
    consisting of your e-mail address;
    change to directory "pub/shuford/terminal" and retrieve one
    or more of the several files for various DEC VTxxx terminals.
    change to directory "pub/shuford/printer" and retrieve one
    or more of the several files for various printers

    (Your computer must have its IP address and domain name registered
    with the Internet DNS for this access to succeed.)

By World Wide Web:

Here is the Uniform Resource Locator from which to start:

    http://www.cs.utk.edu/~shuford/terminal_index.html

The main access method for the collection is still FTP, but the
interaction is made transparent by your web browser.  Make sure
that your browser knows your correct e-mail address.

The collection also includes information on character-cell video
terminals from various other vendors, including IBM, Televideo, and
Wyse, and information on emulations running on personal computers.

I personally wrote very little of the information, and it is of
varying quality, but it has helped some people.


Digital Equipment Corporation has some official information online;
send an e-mail message to <terminals@digital.com>, or use these URLs
on the World Wide Web:

    ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/Digital/info/infosheet/EC-F2423-56.txt

    ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/Digital/termcaps/readme.vt525
    ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/Digital/termcaps/faqvt525.txt
    ftp://ftp.digital.com/pub/Digital/termcaps/vt525seqs.lis

