Pedro M. Matias (matias@itqb.unl.pt) wrote:
: Hi all.

: I have the following problem:
: Two 8mm EXABYTE tapes were written on a UNIX machine using VBACKUP. Back in our
: lab, one of them could be read by VMS BACKUP and the other couldn t. 
: According to the VMS system it has a valid VMS label but it is empty. I suspect
: that the saveset is actually there except there s probably a double tape mark
: at the very beginning of the tape, thus fooling the system into believing it
: is empty.
: Back in the old days of the 9-track reel tape drives, there used to be
: utilities
: that allowed a tape to be read beyond the double tape mark. Is there such an
: utility available nowadays and where can I get it ?

With the tape allocated and mount/foreign, you should be able to do
a set mag/skip=file:1 to skip past the tape mark, and then do a dump
to see what is after.  If you are reading the tape with a utility that
runs with the tape mounted /foreign, just run the utility after issuing
the right number of file mark skips to get to the file label.  If you are
using a utility that needs the tape mounted ANSI (not foreign), then you
need to copy the tape block by block, leaving out the extra file mark.
Then the copied (but modified) tape should work.




