In article <3qku8c$bkl@news.duke.edu> gallatin@hpdemo.isds.duke.edu
(Andrew Gallatin) writes:
> Are there any plans for a Solaris 2.x --> Digital Unix translator?

	We decided to translate Solaris 1.x (a.k.a. SunOS 4.1.x) programs
	because those users are being told by Sun that they will have to
	change operating systems soon anyway.  If they're going to change
	systems, we'd like it to be easier for them to move to Digital Unix
	(a.k.a. Digital OSF/1).

	We heard a lot of feedback at SunWorld that suggested we do a Solaris 2
	translator, and of course that will be factored into our plans of what
	to do next.  As Burkhard points out, many vendors are already native
	on Digital UNIX.  The feedback was mixed, in the sense that many people
	thought we -ought- to do a Solaris 2.x translator, but a fair portion
	of those expressing that opinion said they probably wouldn't use it
	themselves.  I guess it's time to do some *real* market research.

	Meanwhile, we're busy getting the current FreePort Express out.
	I'll remind people that our web page for FreePort Express is

		http://www.novalink.com/freeport-express

> I assume this is a port of mx (DECMigrate)?

	FreePort Express is a descendant of DECmigrate (a.k.a. mx) and also
	of VEST, and the source files show that heritage.  In fact, some of
	the architecture-independent modules are essentially unchanged from
	the last release of mx.  Others are substantially different, and some
	pieces have been written from scratch.

In article <stouffer-0506950853380001@168.143.9.106> stouffer@clark.net
(Edward Stouffer) writes:
>I would assume it is coming from Anton Chernoff's group, who did the
>DECmigrate code since they were working on this and other source platforms
>a year ago. He works for the semiconductor group. He used to live at
>chernoff@kazz.amt.ako.dec.com.

	I wouldn't exactly call it "my" group.  It's the group that I'm in, and
	I've been a substantial consultant and contributor to the project.
	Rick Gorton is the actual project leader and Andy Riebs is the
	manager for FreePort Express.  (Andy writes code for us from time to
	time, too.)

	We are a part of Digital Semiconductor, the people who bring you the
	Alpha chips and other fine silicon-based products.  We are all reachable
	via email to last-name@amt.tay1.dec.com (so my address is thus
	chernoff@amt.tay1.dec.com).

Thanks for your interest, and watch the web page for announcement, technical
notes, and other FreePort Express information.
--
