On 8 Jun 1995, Ong Guan Sin wrote:
> Dear Unix people out there,
> We have a user who cannot log into our Ultrix system. Apparently there are a
> number of idle "login" processes under his uid. The strange thing is that the
> processes cannot be killed even by superuser. "kill -9" under superuser gives
> no error or warning but the procceses stay the same. When I did "su" to his
> uid, my process just hung there. Is it a widely known Unix bug? For your info,
> the system is an Ultrix 4.4 running on a DECsystem 5500. A more general
> question, assuming the statement in man pages that "kill -9 is a sure kill" is
> untrue, is under what circumstances can a process not killable even by
> superuser?
> 
Try:
stop <pid>

where pid is the processes id number of his hung login shell. Once the 
hung login shell is terminated the processes spawned from that should 
also disappear.	If they don't but the login shell does stop, try 
using the kill then. "stop <pid>" Also works to log out users.
