In article <hippo-0606951151110001@cerebus.garply.com>,
Hugh Messenger <hippo@garply.com> wrote:
>A couple of times this month my DNS has gone totally crazy insane.  It
>starts chewing up about 95% of my CPU and completey saturates the network
>with junk traffic.  Killing and restarting the named cures the problem for
>about two minutes.  Rebooting the machine cures it for a week or so.

You may have two nameservers that each think the other is authoritative
for a zone and keep bouncing a DNS request between them.  Given enough
DNS requests, you can beat the system(s) and the network between them
into the ground in no time.  Don't ask me how I know about this...

You could also have a system that's gone nuts and is making DNS requests
as fast as it can; if it's another Alpha, that could be FAST.

You'll need to look at what is going on to see if this is the case.  Do
a "kill -USR1 namedpid" to turn on logging and look at /var/tmp/named.run
(make sure you have a fair amount of disk space just in case).  Look for
repeated DNS requests for the same name (or same domain), and where the
request came from.


