From: ellis@athena.mit.edu (Steve Ellis)
Subject: DEC 5000/25  CPU failure mode
Date: 1995/06/06
Message-ID: <3r1tcg$2g8@senator-bedfellow.MIT.EDU>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 103907153
distribution: world
organization: Massachusetts Institute of Technology
keywords: numerical errors
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec


The Athena computing environment at MIT currently has a large number of
DEC 5000/25 workstations. Recently, a less than desireable CPU failure mode
was found in a small number of these machines. The problem was first 
seen when a third party licensed software package reported license
problems. After a cursory investigation, it was determined that the CPU was bad,
replacing it fixed the problem. After a campus wide power outage, another
machine was also found to have problems with the same third party package.
After a student reported numerical errors on a third machine, a closer look
was taken at the problem, and it was discovered that there were serious
problems with the arithmetic on these machines. In other respects, the machines
appeared to be functioning normally. The arithmetic test program "paranoia"
(available from netlib) was then used to test about 150 workstations.  A total
of 5 machines (including those mentioned above) were found to have arithmetic
flaws. Due to the small number and the sequence of events, I think that the
problem was caused by various power outages experienced here at MIT.
However the subtle nature of the problem leads me to ask what other problems
these machines have.  Does anyone else have any experience with partial CPU
failures on DEC 5000's?  If so, what method or methods did you use to identify 
the problems.


Steve Ellis
ellis@mit.edu



