What I'm trying to point out is that swapping boards is _not_ a good idea 
_unless_ you really know what you are doing. It can cause a lot of 
problems. By all means ask for help. But don't start replacing random 
parts in the hope the fault will vanish. It won't, and the unlucky person who
finally has to get it working will have a much bigger problem to sort out

>is self taught so I sympathise with those who swap boards.  Time is Money!

All my hardware knowledge is self-taught. But I'm quite happy to learn. 
Yes, Time is Money, so it makes sense to get the machine running 
_properly_ again in the shortest possible time. Swap boards at random, 
and you'll never know if an intermittant fault will reappear later. 
Diagnose the fault properly, and you're sure it won't. Since the time 
taken to actually replace the component is minor (<1 minute!) it make 
little difference if you finally replace the board or the component.

