In article <3ql8mn$d16@darkstar.UCSC.EDU>, 
arul@cse.ucsc.edu (Arul Ananthanarayanan) writes...
> 
>Has anyone tried using the ATI Mach64 video card (DEC Part # PB2GA-FA)
>on an Alphastation 250 4/266 running OSF/1? It doesn't appear to be a 
>supported configuration, but is there any reason why this combination 
>wouldn't work?
>

    Yes, the ATI Mach64 card that we qualified on the AlphaStation 200
    4/100 did not meet PCI specification in several areas.  The only
    problem that actually caused a failure in our testbed was that it
    improperly generated parity (setup time violation) on an I/O read.  For
    that reason, we only offer it on our low-end platform, and we disable
    PCI parity checking when that card is present.  To be fair to ATI, they
    are not alone in failing our qualification testing.

    Since virtually all PCI-based systems to date, measured in units sold,
    do not support PCI parity due to a well-known chipset bug, we felt that
    lack of that feature at the bottom of our line was acceptable.  Every
    other system allows PCI parity checking to be enabled by the operator. 
    There are many PCI option cards that do not properly handle parity,
    mainly because market pressures do not force the vendors to test that
    feature rigorously.  We provide a user-selectable option to enable or
    disable the check, so our customers can make their own decision.

    As you knew, you can use an attached terminal as the console of the
    AlphaStation 250.

    -- Kenny

