distribution: world
sender: hobbs@steven.zko.dec.com (Steven Hobbs)
references: <D960KA.Eu0@world.std.com> <3q4h56$je2@post.gsfc.nasa.gov> <PREP.95Jun1013228@yarrow.wt.com.au>
organization: Digital Equipment Corporation
reply-to: hobbs@steven.zent.dec.com (Steven Hobbs)
newsgroups: vmsnet.pdp-11,comp.os.vms,comp.sys.dec


Here is a history of the various BLISSes that I know about.:

BLISS-10 developed at CMU around 1968.  It was written in itself
and was hosted and targeted on the DECsystem-10 and -20.

BLISS-11 developed at CMU around 1972.  It was written in BLISS-10.
It was hosted on the DECsystem-10 and -20 and it targeted the PDP-11.
(General Electric developed a derivative of this compiler that was
hosted on the Honeywell 6000 series).

BLISS-16 (targeted at the PDP-11), BLISS-32 (targeted at the VAX),
and BLISS-36 (targeted at the DECsystem-10 and -20) form Common BLISS.
These compilers were part of a single source pool and they were written
in Common BLISS.  They were hosted on the VAX, DECSystem-10 and -20.
Cross compilers between the VAX and DECsystems were never products although
one customer received an unofficial BLISS-36 that ran on a VAX while
the BLISS-32 that ran on the DECsystems was heavily used internal to Digital
during the early VAX development.  Programs written in the Common BLISS
subset can be compiled by any of these compilers.  These compilers
demonstrate that BLISS code can be portable (although the programmers may
have to work at this a bit more than in other languages).

Micro BLISS-16 was written in BLISS-16 and was a BLISS-16 compiler hosted
on the PDP-11.  Because of the small PDP-11 address space this compiler
did not do very many optimizations.  I do not think that Digital ever
turned this compiler into a product.  It was not used very much internal
to Digital because any project that was targeting the PDP-11 architecture
probably had access to a VAX and the fully optimizing BLISS-16 cross
compiler.

General Electric developed a BLISS-10 that produced Honeywell 6000 code.
It was used to produce the BLISS-11 cross compiler mentioned above.

Bell Labs in Napierville, Illinois developed a BLISS for the IBM 360/370
architecture.  (Not all programmers at Bell Labs liked the C language).

The Soviet Union developed several compilers for BLISS-like languages.

I am certain that there are other BLISS compilers that I do not know about.



