This is all well and good, but how many perfectly competent and
enthusiastic people have got access to a glitch triggered storage scope???
I've always bought the best test & repair gear I could afford, but even a
plain vanilla scope these days is L200+ new, and won't have a bandwidth
sufficient for video/TV repair. If you're prepared to spend the money,
then fine, but to a lot of people computers are the hobby, not
electronics. I'm sorry, but (as has been mentioned before on these groups)
there is an increasing body of people who _never_even_heard_ of Heathkit
doing this sort of thing. 

If you want to buy dead boards off the swappers, repair them and sell them
on back to the swappers at a profit, paying for your time and investment
in gear in the process then go for it. But don't crucify the people who
know their limitations - I've seen people's well meaning attempts at
'repair' and frankly they cause more trouble than they fix. Board
swapping, while not ideal, is far better than random component swapping.

While on the subject, why are technical departments so cautious about
revealing details? So many manufacturers of consumer electronics use
unmarked/unidentifiable components (like nonstandard NiCad battery stacks
with no voltage/capacity markings sealed in plastic so you can't count the
cells (Philips, this means YOU) and then refuse to reveal the contents.
Must we pass a short exam before being admitted to the sacred knowledge?
The number of people willing to dismantle, diagnose and repair their TV 
(for example) can't be so great as to cause significant loss of revenue, 
can it?
 
The only good experience I have had is Marshall Amplification (in the UK) 
who dug out circuit diagrams from 20 years ago for me and gave me 
original component lists (I was trying to undo a botched repair job). 


