True... IMHO a second-hand Tektronix or HP is a lot cheaper and better...

>electronics. I'm sorry, but (as has been mentioned before on these groups)
>there is an increasing body of people who _never_even_heard_ of Heathkit
>doing this sort of thing. 

And unfortunately a lot of them call themselves computer repairers. Now, I
have no objections _at all_ being called by a user/programmer who needs
help in repairing his/her machine (There are plenty of things I can't do,
and I call the appropriate person for help in that case). I'd prefer it if
they didn't 'have a go' first - that causes a lot of problems, many of
which take a very long time to sort out. But I do have great objections to
people who claim to be repairers who do nothing more than swap boards. 

Note that board swapping, at least if done properly, is NOT a trivial 
thing to do. The old board failed for a reason. It might be just age or 
bad luck, or it might be some external cause (PSU, external inputs, 
whatever). In the latter case, which is, alas the more common one in my 
experience, the result is that the new board dies too...

>know their limitations - I've seen people's well meaning attempts at
>'repair' and frankly they cause more trouble than they fix. Board

I know _exactly_ what you mean. But uninformed board swaping also causes all
sorts of problems. My first question on coming to a new repair is 'Has it
_ever_ worked in the current configuation _with the current set of 
boards_'. If not, I try and put the old ones back in (assuming they're still
arround, as then I have the real fault to sort out, and not some possible 
incompatabilities

>swapping, while not ideal, is far better than random component swapping.

Random _anything_ swapping is what I complain about. If the PSU in my PC 
went crazy, then yes, I would replace the cooked motherboard and disk 
controller and video card and whatever. There'd just be too much dead or 
unreliable to attempt a component level repair. But I wouldn't just start 
swapping boards at random until it worked. I'd go through it logically 
with a meter and 'scope and find out what had died. When I find that the 
+5V line is glitching to 20V, and that nothing works, _then_ I replace 
boards.


>While on the subject, why are technical departments so cautious about
>revealing details? So many manufacturers of consumer electronics use

