In article <3qjqp4$1k2@rzsun02.rrz.uni-hamburg.de>, Phillip Helbig 
<phelbig@hs.uni-hamburg.de> writes...
>I would like to have a VERY COARSE BALLPARK FIGURE:
> 
>How does an AlphaStation 200 4/100 compare with
> 
>  i) a VAXSTATION 3100-M76
> 
> ii) a DEC 4000 MODEL 720
> 
>as far as low I/O floating point number crunching
>goes?  Assume each machine has more than enough RAM for 
>the application.  

You said "coarse", right?  OK, the 3100/76 (which by the way I am using
right now as my trusty personal MAIL server) was named the 76 because it
was tested at 7.6 "VUPs" -- that is, 7.6x faster than the 11/780 for a
Digital-internal suite of CPU oriented benchmarks. 

A DEC 4000 Model 720 is a 2-cpu DEC 4000 Model 710.  The 4000/710 is
rated at 185 SPECfp92 -- that is, 185x faster than the 11/780 for a
published, independently verifiable suite of CPU oriented benchmarks.

The VUPs suite cannot be compared with any precision to the SPEC suite,
and was less well controlled (since it was Digital-internal-only).  But
if you want very very rough comparisons, a DEC 4000/710 ought  to crank
about 20x faster than a VAX 3100/76.  A 2-cpu 720 might get 40x if the
job parallelizes well, or if you are running multiple jobs.

By the way, you might also want to check out the AlphaServer 1000.  It
has CPU performance in the same general neighborhood as the 4000/710,
but is is newer, smaller, and uses the Storageworks shelves.  

Hope this helps

