In Article <main.2.0471F2E7@maininc.org>
main@maininc.org (Main Coordination Center) writes:
>We will be upgrading from 5.5 to 6.X.  I have been told that if you go to 
>6.2 you HAVE to go to DECnet phase V.  I have not been able to confirm this.
>
>Can anyone give me or direct me to where I can get some solid info on this?

This is NOT true. It was the plan at one time, but Digital backed off of this
quite a while ago. By default 6.2 comes up with the old, familiar Phase IV.

That said, there is really not too much reason to hold off on Phase V any more.
It's pretty clean and straight-forward to install. It no longer requires the
DECdns nameserver as it now runs from a text file, BIND (the nearly universal
Unix nameserver) or DECdns (or a combination). The management GUI makes the NCL
nightmare go away, and it allows you to run DECnet applications over either the
old Phase IV stack or your favorite IP stack (unless your favorite is CMU). OSI
is still there for anyone who would want such a thing. And it is smart enough
to use Phase IV to nodes only running Phase IV and IP to those runing over IP.

I think it's neat to 
$ COPY ABC.NERSC.GOV::[OBERMAN.JUNK]TESTFILE.DAT SOMEFILE.DAT
and have it work! So don't reject Phase V out of hand.

