Steve Thompson <olin@cheme.cornell.edu> writes:

>On 2 Jun 1995, Luke Brennan wrote:

>I've had a number of both VT2xx's and VT3xx's go up in smoke. Also,
>the VT2xx's power switch seems a favourite point of failure. But the
>cake is taken by the VT100 video monitor board! Probably every VT100
>that I've ever had has had its VMB replaced at some point (that's
>about two dozen of them).


Ones I've had : 

VT300-series (VT320's and VT330's - I know they're very different) seem 
to suffer from flyback transformer failure - often the transformer is 
physically cracked. Replacement transformers are hard to find.

VT220's - quite reliable. I've had one rectifier short, which then blew 
the mains fuse at switch-on. But no other major faults, apart from the 
infamous horizontal hold preset (Screen looses horizontal sync - pull 
cover and turn the HH pot on the video board until it locks. Then set to 
the middle of the locked range, and turn off and on again. Check it locks 
at powerup, and then replace the cover. The correct setting is always 
very near the original point - I guess the pot develops high resistance 
between the wiper and the track)

VT100's suffer from 2 main faults. The first is that capacitors/diodes on 
the video board fail intermittantly, and then kill the flyback 
transformer. A new flyback gets the terminal running again, and then it 
fails again in a couple of weeks. If you replace the flyback it's best to 
change the components in the derrived supplies also.
The second (which doesn't apply to VT101's) is that the PSU chopper 
transistor shorts, and kills the base resistor. This is often caused by 
an open snubber capacitor. After replacing those, the terminal will 
normally work again.



>-steve

-tony




