In article <3qhucj$kgg@gaia.ucs.orst.edu>, rodney@oce.orst.edu (Rodney James) 
writes:
|>Does the standard specify how the merge is implemented?  Is it legal to 
|>evaluate the true and false statements _before_  checking the conditional as 
|>the alpha compiler seems to be doing, or is the alpha compiler generating
|>incorrect code?

As Mike Metcalf says, the standard does not say that the expression is not
evaluated if the mask value is false - the minimal text in the standard
implies to me that it is only the "store" into the function value which is
affected by the mask value.

I have a suspicion that the other compilers are also evaluating the expression
for all values - the difference on the Alpha system is that our default for
exceptions is to report them rather than store an IEEE exceptional value.
Some other systems store the IEEE exceptional value (NaN, Infinity).  You can
get the IEEE exceptional values if you want by compiling with the -fpe1
switch.  I tried this and the program ran to completion.  Our philosophu is
that most users prefer a default which provides the best performance and which
highlights potential program bugs.  If IEEE exceptional values are wanted,
the programmer can easily select that behavior.
